Slovak law does not name hitchhiking (''stopovanie''), so on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://www.slov-lex.sk/pravne-predpisy/SK/ZZ/2009/8/ § 35(1) of Act No. 8/2009 Z. z. on road traffic] puts it the opposite way round from most codes: "only drivers of motor vehicles may use a motorway; for other road users, use of the motorway is prohibited" — a flat ban on pedestrians covering the carriageway and the shoulder, and § 35(2) lets vehicles enter and leave only at the places designated, which takes in the slip road. § 36(1)(a) forbids a driver to stop or stand anywhere on a ''diaľnica'' except a place marked as a car park, apart from emergencies, so a ''parkovisko'' and its petrol station is the only spot on the motorway where a lift can lawfully begin. § 37 carries both rules over to the ''cesta pre motorové vozidlá'', the expressway. Thumb before the motorway sign, or from the verge of a ''cesta I. triedy'' and below, where nothing prohibits it.
